window.docContents[29].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-encodable-vertex-types":{"contents":"So far we have computed greedy colourings of graphs on ℕ. Suppose instead that we\nhave a graph G : SimpleGraph V, where V is countable. Informally we might say\n\"let the vertices of G be v₀, v₁, v₂,…\", implicitly thinking of G as a\nSimpleGraph ℕ. How can we formalize this in Lean?\n\nMathlib has the Encodable type class for constructively countable types (both\nfinite and infinite). It provides an Encodable.encode function that is an injection\nfrom V to ℕ together with a partial inverse Encodable.decode : ℕ → Option V,\nwhich satisfies decode (encode v) = some v for all v : V.\n\n#source Encodable\n\n\nIf G has an encodable vertex type then we can construct a greedy colouring of G\nby first encoding its vertices in ℕ and then applying the greedy colouring\nalgorithm to G as a graph on ℕ. To build the encoded graph we push G forward\nalong Encodable.encode using SimpleGraph.map (Maps).\n\nThe SimpleGraph ℕ obtained from a graph G : SimpleGraph V with encodable\nvertex type V by pushing it forward along Encodable.encode./-- The SimpleGraph ℕ obtained from a graph G : SimpleGraph V with encodable\nvertex type V by pushing it forward along Encodable.encode. -/\n@[simps!]\nprotected def encode {V : Type*} [Encodable V] (G : SimpleGraph V) :\n    SimpleGraph ℕ := G.map Encodable.encode\n\n\nThis captures our informal construction above. To be precise we are not\nclaiming that G.encode is isomorphic to G: in general encode need not be\nsurjective, in which case G.encode will be a copy of G with some additional\nisolated vertices. However, the SimpleGraph.map construction leaves us with\nexistential quantifiers in the adjacency relation.\n\nsection encode\nopen Encodable\nvariable {V : Type*} [Encodable V]\n\nexample {a b : ℕ} {G : SimpleGraph V} : G.encode.Adj a b ↔\n    a ≠ b ∧ ∃ u v, G.Adj u v ∧ encode u = a ∧ encode v = b :=\n  Iff.rfl\n\n\nLean cannot find a DecidableRel instance for this relation, even when G.Adj is\ndecidable, since it has no way to decide the existential over a possibly infinite\nV. We need decidable adjacency to compute colourings, so we supply an instance\nourselves.\n\nGiven [Encodable V] and a : ℕ, the function Encodable.decode₂ V a returns\nsome u such that Encodable.encode u = a, if such a u : V exists, and none\notherwise. So to decide whether a b : ℕ are adjacent in G.encode we can match\non Encodable.decode₂ V a and Encodable.decode₂ V b. If these are some u and\nsome v then a and b are adjacent iff u and v are adjacent in G;\notherwise one of a and b is not the encoding of any vertex, so it is isolated.\n(Plain Encodable.decode would not work here: decode a = some u does not imply\nencode u = a.)\n\n#check decode₂\n\n\nIf G is a graph on an encodable vertex type with decidable adjacency then its\nencoded graph also has decidable adjacency.\n\ninstance {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    DecidableRel G.encode.Adj := fun a b =>\n  match ha : decode₂ V a, hb : decode₂ V b with\n  | some u, some v => decidable_of_iff (G.Adj u v) (by\n      rw [decode₂_eq_some] at ha hb\n      subst ha hb\n      simpa [Relation.Map, encode_injective.eq_iff] using G.ne_of_adj)\n  | none, _ => isFalse fun ⟨_, u, _, _, hu, _⟩ => by simp [← hu] at ha\n  | _, none => isFalse fun ⟨_, _, v, _, _, hv⟩ => by simp [← hv] at hb\n\n\nWe made SimpleGraph.encode a def rather than an abbrev so that this is the\nonly instance. Mathlib already has a DecidableRel instance for G.map f when V\nis finite, which decides the existential by checking every vertex. Instance search\nsees through an abbrev, so for finite V that instance would compete with ours,\nbut it does not unfold a def.\n\nTo transfer a greedy colouring of the graph G.encode back to a colouring of G we\nuse an embedding of G into G.encode. Mathlib already provides one: for any\nembedding f : V ↪ W, Embedding.map f G embeds G into G.map f, and\nEncodable.encode' V is Encodable.encode bundled as an embedding V ↪ ℕ.\n\n#check Embedding.map\n#check encode'\n\n\nThe graph embedding of G into G.encode via Encodable.encode./-- The graph embedding of G into G.encode via Encodable.encode. -/\ndef Embedding.encode (G : SimpleGraph V) : G ↪g G.encode :=\n  Embedding.map (encode' V) G\n\n\nWe would like to prove the greedy colouring bound for any graph of bounded degree on\nan Encodable, or even a plain Countable, vertex type.\n\nWe need a couple of simple lemmas. Firstly the earlier neighbourhood of an encoded\nvertex in the encoded graph is contained in the image of the neighbourhood of that\nvertex in the original graph under the encoding.\n\nlemma neighborLTFinset_encode_subset {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    [G.LocallyFinite] (v : V) : G.encode.neighborLTFinset (encode v)\n      ⊆ (G.neighborFinset v).image encode := by\n  intro m hm\n  obtain ⟨_, u, w, h, hu, rfl⟩ := ((mem_neighborLTFinset ..).mp hm).2\n  exact mem_image_of_mem _ (by simpa [encode_injective hu] using h)\n\n\nHence the degreeLT of an encoded vertex is at most the degree of the original\nvertex.\n\nlemma degreeLT_encode_le {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (v : V) : G.encode.degreeLT (encode v) ≤ G.degree v := by\n  rw [degreeLT, degree]\n  exact (card_le_card (neighborLTFinset_encode_subset v)).trans card_image_le\n\n\nUsing these lemmas we can now give the greedy (Δ + 1)-colouring of a graph with\nmaximum degree Δ on an encodable vertex type with decidable adjacency. (Note that\nwe don't actually refer to maxDegree below, since this would require a\nmathematically unnecessary Fintype V instance. Instead we assume that all vertices\nhave finite neighbourhoods using G.LocallyFinite and a bound Δ : ℕ such that\n∀ v, G.degree v ≤ Δ.)\n\nThe greedy Fin (Δ + 1) colouring of a graph with bounded degree Δ./-- The greedy Fin (Δ + 1) colouring of a graph with bounded degree Δ. -/\ndef greedyColoringFin {Δ : ℕ} {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    [G.LocallyFinite] (hdeg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ Δ) : G.Coloring (Fin (Δ + 1)) where\n  toFun v := ⟨G.encode.greedy (encode v), by\n    grind [G.degreeLT_encode_le v, G.encode.greedy_lt (encode v)]⟩\n  map_rel' h := Fin.ne_of_val_ne ((Embedding.encode G).map_rel_iff.mpr h).greedy_ne\n\nend encode\n\n\nWe can also prove (classically) that such a colouring exists without any assumptions\nabout encodability or decidability. For this a Countable instance, together with\nthe degree bound, suffices.\n\ntheorem colorable_of_degree_le_of_countable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} {Δ : ℕ}\n    [Countable V] [G.LocallyFinite] (hdeg : ∀ v, G.degree v ≤ Δ) :\n    G.Colorable (Δ + 1) := by\n  classical\n  rcases nonempty_encodable V with ⟨_⟩\n  exact ⟨greedyColoringFin hdeg⟩\n\n\nLater we will use the De Bruijn–Erdős\ntheorem to show that the countable assumption is also superfluous, but for\nthis we need to understand Subgraphs.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*","header":"7.8.5. Encodable Vertex Types","id":"/Colorings/#section-encodable-vertex-types"},"/Constructions/#section-complete-bipartite-graphs":{"contents":"Given two disjoint vertex sets V and W we can form the complete bipartite graph\nwith classes V and W. This seems to involve the idea that V and W are\ndisjoint, but in Lean we are working with types not sets and so have no way of\ntalking about disjointness of two arbitrary types.\n\nLean does however have the very useful Sum type, written V ⊕ W, which plays the\nrole of the disjoint union of types V and W. Note that this is not the same as\nthe set-theoretic union: any x : V ⊕ W \"knows\" which of the two sides of the sum it\ncame from. It is either Sum.inl v for some v : V, or Sum.inr w for some\nw : W. In particular we can form the disjoint union of a type V with itself as\nV ⊕ V.\n\n#source Sum\nexample {V : Type*} (v : V) : V ⊕ V := Sum.inl v\n\n\nThis gives a natural way to define complete bipartite graphs.\n\n#source completeBipartiteGraph\n\n\nThe complete bipartite graph K_{4,5}\n\nWe introduce notation for the canonical complete bipartite graph with parts Fin s\nand Fin t.\n\nK[s, t] is the complete bipartite graph on Fin s ⊕ Fin t./-- K[s, t] is the complete bipartite graph on Fin s ⊕ Fin t. -/\nscoped notation \"K[\" s \", \" t \"]\" => completeBipartiteGraph (Fin s) (Fin t)\n\n\n#check K[4, 5]  -- K[4, 5] : SimpleGraph (Fin 4 ⊕ Fin 5)\n\n\nIf we try to check adjacency of two vertices in K[4, 5] we get an error.\n\n/- error: failed to synthesize\n Decidable (K[4, 5].Adj (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inr 3))\n-/\n#eval K[4, 5].Adj (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inr 3)\n\n\nThis is because there is currently no instance of decidable adjacency for\ncompleteBipartiteGraph in Mathlib. In this case it is easy to give. (Don't worry if\nthis is unfamiliar; we will look at decidability in much more detail in\nDecidability, Finiteness and Degrees.)\n\ninstance (V W : Type*) : DecidableRel (completeBipartiteGraph V W).Adj := by\n  intro v w\n  unfold completeBipartiteGraph\n  infer_instance\n\n\nexample : K[4, 5].Adj (Sum.inl 0) (Sum.inr 3) := by decide\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Basic Families of Graphs","header":"3.1.5. Complete Bipartite Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-complete-bipartite-graphs"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-darts":{"contents":"Since edges in SimpleGraph are undirected it might seem strange to introduce the\nnotion of a dart (an oriented edge). However we already noted that h : G.Adj u v\nhas a direction to it; darts simply give us a way to talk about the ordered pairs of\nadjacent vertices in a graph. These can be very useful, for example in double\ncounting arguments such as proving the handshake lemma.\n\nA dart d : G.Dart is an ordered pair of vertices together with a proof that they\nare adjacent in G. Since edges are unoriented, darts come in symmetric pairs: if\nd : G.Dart then d.symm is the opposite dart and d.edge is the edge\ncorresponding to d.\n\nsection darts\n\n#source Dart\n\n\nvariable (d : G.Dart)\n#check d.fst  -- the first vertex of the dart\n#check d.snd  -- the second vertex of the dart\n#check d.adj  -- the adjacency proof of the dart\n#check d.edge -- the edge corresponding to the dart\n#check d.symm -- the opposite dart from d.snd to d.fst\n\n\nEdges are unoriented so d and d.symm correspond to the same edge.\n\nexample : d.symm.edge = d.edge :=\n  Dart.edge_symm d\n\n\nexample : d.fst = d.symm.snd := rfl\n\nend darts\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.4. Darts","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-darts"}});